# Line-ending policy.
#
# This file exists so that a machine's checkout setting cannot change what the
# repository contains, or what the project's own checks see.
#
# Why it is needed: the project is developed on Windows, where git's
# core.autocrlf rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. Two of the project's checks
# compare a file on disk against content the project generates, and both were
# fooled by that rewrite:
#
#   tools/export-database-dump.mjs --check
#       compared database/charityevents_db.sql against the file it would
#       assemble, and reported STALE on any machine whose checkout differed
#       from the one that generated it.
#
#   The inline theme snippet in each index.html (the code that stops a dark-mode
#   visitor seeing a white flash). It used to live in the vanilla js/theme.js and
#   be copied into the pages by tools/sync-theme-snippet.mjs, which reported every
#   page as OUT OF DATE for the same reason. Both the file and the tool are gone -
#   the snippet is now guarded by tools/check-angular-architecture.mjs, which
#   compares it against shared/src/theme/theme.service.ts - but the pages are
#   still read, so the pin still matters.
#
# In both cases the content was correct and only the line endings differed.
# Pinning the files these checks read makes the comparison independent of the
# checkout. The blobs in the repository are already LF, so this adds no churn;
# it only stops the working copy from being rewritten.

# The database scripts and the dump assembled from them.
database/*.sql text eol=lf

# The pages, the templates and the shared assets.
*.html text eol=lf

# TypeScript: both Angular applications, the shared library and the API.
# Pinned because the languages are compared as text by tools/check-translations
# .mjs (the dictionaries) and by tsconfig-driven builds, and because a mixed
# working copy made the API migration's diffs noisy.
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.mts  text eol=lf
*.cts  text eol=lf

# The JavaScript and TypeScript tooling.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf

# Documentation and PowerShell scripts.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=lf

# Data files read by the tooling.
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# Batch files are Windows-only, so they keep Windows endings.
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never rewritten.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.zip  binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf  binary